%description:

RFC 9000 -- flow control.

Checks RFC9000-FC-1 (must), from doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc9000/catalog.md. The English
procedure and mockup are in doc/project/evidence/protocol/quic/checks/flow-control.md#flow-control; the program
steps below map one to one onto its expected observations. Mockup mapping: host A = client, host B
= server.

Scenario: host B's (the server's) initial stream flow-control limit is set to 2000 octets
(`**.server.quic.initialMaxStreamData = 2000B` -- the parameter is declared `int
initialMaxStreamData @unit(B)` in src/inet/transportlayer/quic/Quic.ned, so a bare byte literal is
the right unit), smaller than the 4000-octet block, so the limit binds (checks.md's own scenario
constants for this check). `sendMaxDataFramesImmediately` and `roundConsumedDataValue` are also set
on host B, mirroring examples/quic/flow_control_limited/omnetpp.ini's own working configuration of
the same mechanism, so the receiver raises the limit as soon as it has consumed enough rather than
waiting to piggyback the MAX_STREAM_DATA frame on some other outgoing packet. Host A otherwise uses
the same 4000-octet, one-shot, non-closing TrafficgenCompound+QuicTrafficgen scenario as
Rfc9000ConnectionEstablishment.test.

Implementation of observation 2 ("the total stream data host A sends, before any frame from host B
raises the limit, does not exceed 2000 octets"): the task brief suggested either a `never` step for
an over-limit STREAM frame before the first MAX_STREAM_DATA, or a sum accumulated in a lambda.
Neither is directly usable as first imagined, for reasons specific to this engine (confirmed by
reading tests/protocol/lib/ProtocolTester.cc/EventPattern.cc, not just guessed):
  - The engine used to be strictly sequential and single-pass: one step was current at a
    time, and an event a step did not match was gone. So the rule could not be a `never`
    step. Any window long enough to be robust ran past the point the next step had to
    observe, and the gap between host A's initial burst and host B's first MAX_STREAM_DATA
    is under 200 microseconds here. The check therefore threaded mutable state through every
    step's predicate.

    **That is no longer so, and this check is written the way the rule reads.** `meanwhile`
    starts a guard without waiting for it, and one event now reaches every running step, so
    the rule is a guard that runs beside the ordered steps and reports a violation at the
    moment it happens. The guard was verified to fire: with the limit lowered to 100 octets
    it reports "forbidden event occurred at t=0.1".

  - One limit still holds. A predicate cannot signal failure by throwing:
    `EventPattern::selectorMatches` wraps every `filterEvent` call in a try/catch that turns
    any exception into a silent non-match, by design, so that a predicate peeking a field
    absent from an unrelated packet fails safe.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "QuicChunks.h"
#include <memory>

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

// The limit host B advertises for the stream, in octets. RFC 9000 section 4.1 binds the
// sender to it: host A may not send past it until host B raises it.
static const long STREAM_LIMIT = 2000;

// True once host B has raised the limit. The guard below forbids a send past the limit only
// while this is false, which is what "until it is raised" means.
static bool limitRaised = false;

// Runs on every datagram host A receives, and never matches: it only follows the stream.
static bool followMaxStreamData(const MatchContext& context)
{
    if (context.event.packet != nullptr && findQuicChunk<MaxStreamDataFrameHeader>(context.event.packet))
        limitRaised = true;
    return false;
}

// True when host A sends past the limit while the limit still stands. This is the violation
// RFC9000-FC-1 forbids, and the guard reports it the moment it happens.
static bool sentPastTheLimit(const MatchContext& context)
{
    if (limitRaised || context.event.packet == nullptr)
        return false;
    auto frame = findQuicChunk<StreamFrameHeader>(context.event.packet);
    return frame != nullptr && (long)(frame->getOffset() + frame->getLength()) > STREAM_LIMIT;
}

static bool streamFrame(const MatchContext& context)
{
    return context.event.packet != nullptr
        && findQuicChunk<StreamFrameHeader>(context.event.packet) != nullptr;
}

static bool maxStreamDataFrame(const MatchContext& context)
{
    return context.event.packet != nullptr
        && findQuicChunk<MaxStreamDataFrameHeader>(context.event.packet) != nullptr;
}

static bool completesTheBlock(const MatchContext& context)
{
    if (context.event.packet == nullptr)
        return false;
    auto frame = findQuicChunk<StreamFrameHeader>(context.event.packet);
    return frame != nullptr && frame->getOffset() + frame->getLength() == 4000;
}

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc9000_flow_control")
        // Follow host B's answers for the whole run, so the guard knows when the limit was
        // raised. This guard never matches; it only records.
        .meanwhile(never(on("client.udp").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                             .filterEvent(followMaxStreamData)
                             .describe("host B raising the limit")
                             .within(5.0)))
        // The rule itself, as a guard rather than as a condition threaded through the other
        // steps: host A must not send past the limit while the limit stands. It runs beside
        // every step below, so a violation at any moment ends the run and names itself.
        .meanwhile(never(on("client.udp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                             .filterEvent(sentPastTheLimit)
                             .describe("a STREAM frame past the limit, before host B raised it")
                             .within(5.0)))
        // Host A sends a STREAM frame. This confirms the stimulus, so the guards hold over
        // a run in which something happened.
        .once(on("client.udp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterEvent(streamFrame)
                  .describe("host A's first STREAM frame")
                  .within(0.5))
        // Host B raises the limit.
        .once(on("client.udp").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                  .filterEvent(maxStreamDataFrame)
                  .describe("host B's MAX_STREAM_DATA, raising the limit")
                  .within(2.0))
        // And only then does host A send the remainder, completing the 4000 octet block.
        .once(on("client.udp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterEvent(completesTheBlock)
                  .describe("host A sends the remainder, completing all 4000 octets")
                  .within(2.0));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc9000_flow_control compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// Mockup of doc/project/evidence/protocol/quic/checks.md's "Common mockup":
// host A (client) -- path P -- host B (server).
network QuicNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        client: StandardHost { @display("p=250,100"); }
        server: StandardHost { @display("p=450,100"); }
    connections:
        client.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> server.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = QuicNet
sim-time-limit = 5s
seed-set = 0

**.quic.typename = "Quic"

# client: a single 4000-octet burst on one stream (checks.md's common-mockup data block).
**.client.hasQuic = true
**.client.numApps = 1
**.client.app[0].typename = "TrafficgenCompound"
**.client.app[0].numGenerators = 1
**.client.app[0].generator[0].typename = "TrafficgenSimple"
**.client.app[0].generator[0].id = 0
**.client.app[0].generator[0].packetSize = 4000B
**.client.app[0].generator[0].packetCount = -1
**.client.app[0].generator[0].packetInterval = 10s
**.client.app[0].generator[0].startTime = 0.1s
**.client.app[0].generator[0].ordered = false
**.client.app[0].handler.typename = "QuicTrafficgen"
**.client.app[0].handler.localPort = 1001
**.client.app[0].handler.connectPort = 1000
**.client.app[0].handler.connectAddress = "server"

# server: reads and discards everything it receives, with its initial stream flow-control
# limit set below the 4000-octet block so the limit binds (checks.md's scenario constants).
**.server.hasQuic = true
**.server.numApps = 1
**.server.app[*].typename = "QuicDiscardServer"
**.server.app[*].localAddress = ""
**.server.app[*].localPort = 1000
**.server.quic.initialMaxStreamData = 2000B
**.server.quic.sendMaxDataFramesImmediately = true
**.server.quic.roundConsumedDataValue = true

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc9000_flow_control: PASS
